{"conjecture": "Long-running software systems degrade not because individual changes are wrong, but because each locally-correct modification permanently shrinks the space of future valid changes, creating an entropy-like quantity that grows monotonically with every commit. This 'architectural entropy' is invisible to individual code review because it is a property of the relationships between changes over time, not of any single change itself. The system becomes harder to modify because the accumulating constraints from past locally-correct decisions progressively narrow the set of modifications that remain consistent with the existing design, until the cost of navigating this constraint space exceeds the cost of the actual change being made."}
